\"), but test asserts normalize_team(\"Palmeiras-SP\") == normalize_team(\"Palmeiras\"). team_keys(\"Palmeiras\") = {\"palmeiras\"} but normalize_team(\"Palmeiras\") = \"palmeiras\" while normalize_team(\"Palmeiras-SP\") = \"palmeiras sp\" — NOT equal. Hmm.\n\nDecision: normalize_team should return the canonical grouping key. For name-variation matching, the question is whether query without state should equal the state-suffixed canonical. Two options:\na) canonical = base (drop state) — but then Atletico-MG/PR/GO merge.\nb) canonical = state-suffixed when known — query without state won't string-equal.\n\nBetter: make normalize_team return base-with-alias-representative, i.e. drop state, EXCEPT for ambiguous bases where state matters. Too fiddly. Alternative: keep canonical = min over full keyset? For \"Palmeiras-SP\": keyset {\"palmeiras\",\"palmeiras sp\"} → min = \"palmeiras\". For \"Palmeiras\": {\"palmeiras\"} → \"palmeiras\". Equal! For \"Atletico-MG\": keyset {\"atletico\", \"atletico mg\"} → min \"atletico\". But that merges Atletico teams in normalize_team equality — however standings grouping must use match_key with state.\n\nHonestly the cleanest semantics:\n- `normalize_team` = fuzzy key used for matching: min(team_keys) — gives \"palmeiras\" == \"palmeiras\" ✓, \"corinthians\" ✓, \"se palmeiras\" ✓.\n- `group_key` = unambiguous identity: state-suffixed when state present, alias-representative otherwise: \"atletico mg\", \"palmeiras sp\", \"palmeiras\" (no state).\n\nMatching (same_team) uses keyset intersection. Grouping (standings, records) uses group_key.\n\nUpdate: define group_key separately in normalize_team... Let me add `group_key(name)`:\n```python\ndef group_key(name):\n    tokens, state = _base_and_state(name)\n    base = \" \".join(tokens)\n    key = f\"{base} {state}\".strip() if state else base\n    return min(_ALIAS_LOOKUP.get(key, {key}))\n```\nThat's what my current normalize_team does. So rename: current normalize_team → group_key, and normalize_team = min(team_keys(name)).\n\nBut analysis uses keyset intersection for query matching (good) and group_key for aggregation. In _record and team_stats, determining \"is team X the home side\": use same_team(query, m.home)? Query \"Atletico-MG\" vs match home \"Atletico-MG\" — intersect ✓. Query \"Atlético Mineiro\" keys {\"atletico mineiro\",\"atletico mg\"...} vs home \"Atletico-MG\" keys {\"atletico\",\"atletico mg\",...} — intersect on \"atletico mg\" ✓. But query \"Atletico\" (ambiguous) would match all three Atleticos — acceptable.\n\nBUT stats record computation: if I compute record for query \"Atletico-MG\" using matches found via keys {\"atletico\",\"atletico mg\"}, matches_for_team returns matches for BOTH Atletico-PR and Atletico-MG (via base \"atletico\"). Then _record must attribute only matches where the team side matches precisely. Use group_key precision: filter matches where group_key(m.home) == group_key(query)? group_key(\"Atlético Mineiro\") = \"atletico mineiro\"... wait group_key returns min of alias set = \"atletico mg\" ✓ (min of {\"atletico mg\",\"atletico mineiro\"} is \"atletico mg\"). group_key(\"Atletico-MG\") = \"atletico mg\" ✓. Good, they agree.\n\nBut query \"Palmeiras\" group_key = \"palmeiras\", while match name \"Palmeiras-SP\" group_key = \"palmeiras sp\" ≠. Ugh.\n\nOK simplest robust approach for _record: attribute home/away by same_team AND for ambiguous bases require group compatibility... Let's simplify: _record attribute side by checking same_team(team_query, m.home) — for query \"Atletico-MG\", matches_for_team returns PR/MG/GO matches; same_team(\"Atletico-MG\", \"Atletico-PR\")? team_keys(\"Atletico-MG\") = {\"atletico\",\"atletico mg\"}+aliases{\"atletico mineiro\"}; team_keys(\"Atletico-PR\") = {\"atletico\",\"atletico pr\",\"atletico paranaense\",\"athletico paranaense\"}. Intersection = {\"atletico\"} — non-empty! So same_team says true. Bad.\n\nFix: team_keys should NOT include the bare base when the base is ambiguous (belongs to multiple alias groups / state variants). But we don't know variants at name level... We can preprocess: collect all group_keys; a base is ambiguous if multiple group_keys share that base. Then team_keys excludes base only if ambiguous.\n\nImplement in SoccerData init: build ambiguity set = bases mapping to >1 distinct group_keys across all matches. Then provide `team_keys(name, ambiguous_bases)`? Circular. Alternative: compute team_keys lazily with a module-level registry updated at load time. Pragmatic: hardcode known ambiguous bases: \"atletico\", \"america\" (America-MG vs America-RN?... in these files \"America-MG\" only; but BR-Football has \"America (MG)\"? whatever), \"sport\"? Sport-PE unique; \"palmeiras\" unique. Hardcode AMBIGUOUS_BASES = {\"atletico\", \"america\", \"nacional\", \"internacional\"? no...}. In our data: atletico (MG/PR/GO), america (America-MG; also \"América - MG\" in Copa file — same). Let me check actual ambiguous bases across data quickly, then hardcode a set with the discovered ones (plus atletico). Reasonable for a demo.\n\nThen team_keys: include base only if base not in AMBIGUOUS_BASES. normalize_team = min(team_keys). same_team via intersection.\n\nLet me now check ambiguous bases in the data.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05d96ba40001DF5JcjI2h9uH5A","sessionID":"ses_fa26fadadffe2UVfX81vYXdx41","messageID":"msg_05d967820001bbG0ekBiP2BrAU"}}
