ue when two raw team names refer to the same club.\"\"\"\n-    return bool(team_keys(a) & team_keys(b))\n+    return bool(_bare_keys(a) & _bare_keys(b))\n \n \n def parse_date(value: str) -> date | None:\n     \"\"\"Parse the date formats used across the datasets.\"\"\"\n","additions":16,"deletions":15},"truncated":false},"title":"brazilian_soccer/loader.py","time":{"start":1788276566351,"end":1788276566353}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Truly ambiguous bases (same base, different states) in Serie A context: atletico (GO/MG/PR/ES/AC), america (MG/RN), botafogo (PB/RJ), bragantino (PA/SP), internacional (RS/SC), juventude (MA/RS), santos (AP/SP), flamengo (PI/RJ — Serie A \"Flamengo-RJ\" only), guarani, nacional, nautico, operario, santa cruz, vitoria etc. Many Copa do Brasil small clubs.\n\nDesign decision: bare base included in keyset only when unambiguous... but computing ambiguity dynamically is the robust way. Do it in SoccerData: after loading, compute base→set(group_keys); bases with >1 group are \"ambiguous\". Then matches_for_team / comparisons should use group-key-aware matching.\n\nSimpler final approach — change matching semantics:\n- `group_key(name)`: state-suffixed canonical (or alias rep). Unique per club variant.\n- `team_keys(name)`: {group_key} ∪ {base} ∪ alias members ∪ ... but base included only if SoccerData says base unambiguous.\n\nMake team_keys take optional `ambiguous_bases` set. Module-level default = known list (empty). SoccerData computes and passes it. But analysis calls team_keys freely... Instead: make SoccerData own a `keys_for(name)` method that excludes ambiguous bases, and analysis functions receive data → use data.keys_for. Same_team in loader used only by tests? I defined same_team in loader; keep it using full keysets (with base always) for generic use, but analysis uses data.keys_for.\n\nActually cleaner: keep module-level AMBIGUOUS_BASES set computed lazily? The ambiguity is data-dependent; compute inside SoccerData and expose `data.keys_for(name)` and `data.group_key(name)`. Update analysis to use `data.keys_for`.\n\nAnalysis changes:\n- find_matches: team_keys = data.keys_for(team); home side check `data.keys_for(m.home) & team_keys`.\n- team_stats/_record: same.\n- head_to_head: both sides.\n- standings: group by data.group_key(m.home).\n- search_players club: use data.keys_for(club) & data.keys_for(p.club).\n- players_at_club: index built with data.keys_for.\n\nRewrite loader SoccerData accordingly and analysis. Let me code it.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05d96dadd001mP7Kv9dZvT1r6j","sessionID":"ses_fa26fadadffe2UVfX81vYXdx41","messageID":"msg_05d96c19e001CTYcIjbG5T7p8N"}}
