{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788276668729,"sessionID":"ses_fa26fadadffe2UVfX81vYXdx41","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"edit","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-b89505de7491d010","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-hsquwan9/retort-2debf171a916/brazilian_soccer/loader.py","newString":"        # A bare base name (state suffix stripped) is ambiguous when it is\n        # shared by clubs from more than one distinct state (e.g.\n        # \"atletico\": MG/PR/GO, \"botafogo\": RJ/PB). A base seen both with\n        # and without a suffix (\"Palmeiras\", \"Palmeiras-SP\") is NOT\n        # ambiguous — that is the same club spelled two ways.\n        base_states: dict[str, set[str]] = {}\n        for m in matches:\n            for name in (m.home, m.away):\n                tokens, state = _base_and_state(name)\n                base = \" \".join(tokens)\n                if base:\n                    base_states.setdefault(base, set()).add(state)\n        self.ambiguous_bases = {\n            base\n            for base, states in base_states.items()\n            if len(states - {\"\"}) > 1\n        }\n        self._base_groups: dict[str, set[str]] = {}\n        for m in matches:\n            for name in (m.home, m.away):\n                tokens, _ = _base_and_state(name)\n                base = \" \".join(tokens)\n                if base:\n                    self._base_groups.setdefault(base, set()).add(group_key(name))","oldString":"        # A bare base name (state suffix stripped) that maps to more than\n        # one distinct club (e.g. \"atletico\": MG/PR/GO, \"botafogo\": RJ/PB)\n        # must not be used as a lookup key on its own.\n        base_groups: dict[str, set[str]] = {}\n        for m in matches:\n            for name in (m.home, m.away):\n                tokens, state = _base_and_state(name)\n                base = \" \".join(tokens)\n                if base:\n                    base_groups.setdefault(base, set()).add(group_key(name))\n        self.ambiguous_bases = {\n            base for base, groups in base_groups.items() if len(groups) > 1\n        }"},"output":"Edit applied successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"diff":"Index: /Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-hsquwan9/retort-2debf171a916/brazilian_soccer/loader.py\n===================================================================\n--- /Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-hsquwan9/retort-2debf171a916/brazilian_soccer/loader.py\n+++ /Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-hsquwan9/retort-2debf171a916/brazilian_soccer/loader.py\n@@ -263,21 +263,32 @@\n \n def __init__(self, matches: list[Match], players: list[Player]):\n     self.matches = matches\n     self.players = players\n-    # A bare base name (state suffix stripped) that maps to more than\n-    # one distinct club (e.g. \"atletico\": MG/PR/GO, \"botafogo\": RJ/PB)\n-    # must not be used as a lookup key on its own.\n-    base_groups: dict[str, set[str]] = {}\n+    # A bare base name (state suffix stripped) is ambiguous when it is\n+    # shared by clubs from more than one distinct state (e.g.\n+    # \"atletico\": MG/PR/GO, \"botafogo\": RJ/PB). A base seen both with\n+    # and without a suffix (\"Palmeiras\", \"Palmeiras-SP\") is NOT\n+    # ambiguous — that is the same club spelled two ways.\n+    base_states: dict[str, set[str]] = {}\n     for m in matches:\n         for name in (m.home, m.away):\n             tokens, state = _base_and_state(name)\n             base = \" \".join(tokens)\n             if base:\n-                base_groups.setdefault(base, set()).add(group_key(name))\n+                base_states.setdefault(base, set()).add(state)\n     self.ambiguous_bases = {\n-        base for base, groups in base_groups.items() if len(groups) > 1\n+        base\n+        for base, states in base_states.items()\n+        if len(states - {\"\"}) > 1\n     }\n+    self._base_groups: dict[str, set[str]] = {}\n+    for m in matches:\n+        for name in (m.home, m.away):\n+            tokens, _ = _base_and_state(name)\n+            base = \" \".join(tokens)\n+            if base:\n+                self._base_groups.setdefault(base, set()).add(group_key(name))\n     self._match_index: dict[str, list[Match]] = {}\n     self._player_club_index: dict[str, list[Player]] = {}\n     for m in matches:\n         for key in self.keys_for(m.home):\n","filediff":{"file":"/Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-hsquwan9/retort-2debf171a916/brazilian_soccer/loader.py","patch":"Index: /Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-hsquwan9/retort-2debf171a916/brazilian_soccer/loader.py\n===================================================================\n--- /Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-hsquwan9/retort-2debf171a916/brazilian_soccer/loader.py\n+++ /Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-hsquwan9/retort-2debf171a916/brazilian_soccer/loader.py\n@@ -263,21 +263,32 @@\n \n def __init__(self, matches: list[Match], players: list[Player]):\n     self.matches = matches\n     self.players = players\n-    # A bare base name (state suffix stripped) that maps to more than\n-    # one distinct club (e.g. \"atletico\": MG/PR/GO, \"botafogo\": RJ/PB)\n-    # must not be used as a lookup key on its own.\n-    base_groups: dict[str, set[str]] = {}\n+    # A bare base name (state suffix stripped) is ambiguous when it is\n+    # shared by clubs from more than one distinct state (e.g.\n+    # \"atletico\": MG/PR/GO, \"botafogo\": RJ/PB). A base seen both with\n+    # and without a suffix (\"Palmeiras\", \"Palmeiras-SP\") is NOT\n+    # ambiguous — that is the same club spelled two ways.\n+    base_states: dict[str, set[str]] = {}\n     for m in matches:\n         for name in (m.home, m.away):\n             tokens, state = _base_and_state(name)\n             base = \" \".join(tokens)\n             if base:\n-                base_groups.setdefault(base, set()).add(group_key(name))\n+                base_states.setdefault(base, set()).add(state)\n     self.ambiguous_bases = {\n-        base for base, groups in base_groups.items() if len(groups) > 1\n+        base\n+        for base, states in base_states.items()\n+        if len(states - {\"\"}) > 1\n     }\n+    self._base_groups: dict[str, set[str]] = {}\n+    for m in matches:\n+        for name in (m.home, m.away):\n+            tokens, _ = _base_and_state(name)\n+            base = \" \".join(tokens)\n+            if base:\n+                self._base_groups.setdefault(base, set()).add(group_key(name))\n     self._match_index: dict[str, list[Match]] = {}\n     self._player_club_index: dict[str, list[Player]] = {}\n     for m in matches:\n         for key in self.keys_for(m.home):\n","additions":17,"deletions":6},"truncated":false},"title":"brazilian_soccer/loader.py","time":{"start":1788276668726,"end":1788276668728}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"So base \"palmeiras\" has multiple group_keys: \"palmeiras\" (from \"Palmeiras\", no state) and \"palmeiras sp\" (from \"Palmeiras-SP\"). group_key(\"Palmeiras\") = \"palmeiras\" (no state) ≠ group_key(\"Palmeiras-SP\") = \"palmeiras sp\". So ambiguity detection counts these as different clubs — wrong, they're the same club; the unsuffixed one just lacks state info.\n\nFix: group_key should drop the state when the (base+state) is unambiguous in data... circular again. Better: group identity = base + state when present, but ambiguity detection should treat \"base\" and \"base state\" as the same club unless another state exists for the same base. I.e., ambiguity = base maps to >1 distinct STATE (other than empty). Compute: for each base, set of states; if states - {\"\"} has >1 element → ambiguous. If a base has states {\"\", \"sp\"} → unambiguous (the \"\" is just missing suffix).\n\nBut then group_key(\"Palmeiras\") = \"palmeiras\" vs group_key(\"Palmeiras-SP\") = \"palmeiras sp\" still differ for standings grouping! Standings within one file consistently uses suffixed names, so grouping by group_key works (all \"Palmeiras-SP\"). But cross-checks like h2h use keysets which include base when unambiguous. So make keys_for: keys = {group_key(name)} ∪ ({base} if base unambiguous) ∪ aliases...\n\nAnd group_key itself: if base unambiguous in data... group_key is a pure module function without data. For standings grouping, better to have data.group_of(name) = state-suffixed key if name has state else... For names without state in the same competition set, they group by base — consistent within a file.\n\nActually for standings: all names in Brasileirão file have state suffixes, so group_key works. For BR-Football \"Serie A\" (no suffixes) group_key = base — but each club appears with one spelling, fine.\n\nThe real fix is only in ambiguity computation: count distinct non-empty states per base. Let me change:\n\n```python\nbase_states: dict[str, set[str]] = {}\nfor name: tokens, state = _base_and_state(name); base_states.setdefault(base,set()).add(state)\nself.ambiguous_bases = {b for b, sts in base_states.items() if len(sts - {\"\"}) > 1}\n```\n\n\"atletico\" states {mg, pr, go, es, ac} → ambiguous ✓. \"palmeiras\" {\"\", \"sp\"} → unambiguous ✓. \"flamengo\" {\"\", \"rj\", \"pi\"} → ambiguous (Flamengo-PI exists in Copa file!). Hmm — then query \"Flamengo\" gets only keys {\"flamengo rj\"}? No — keys_for(\"Flamengo\") where \"flamengo\" ambiguous → bare base excluded → keys = group_key(\"flamengo\") = \"flamengo\" only... group_key has no state → \"flamengo\" — excluded but it IS a key itself. keys = _bare_keys minus base... \"flamengo\" removed → empty → fallback {group_key} = \"flamengo\". Which matches only unsuffixed \"flamengo\" matches. Bad: query Flamengo should match Flamengo-RJ primarily.\n\nBetter fallback: when the bare base is ambiguous but the query has no state, prefer the MOST COMMON group (Flamengo-RJ over Flamengo-PI). Implement: in keys_for, if base ambiguous and no state in the name, include all group variants? That would return Flamengo-PI matches too (a tiny Piaui club) — slightly noisy but acceptable? For stats \"Flamengo\" in Brasileirão season it would only count RJ matches (PI club doesn't play Brasileirão Serie A years covered). find_matches team=Flamengo without competition → includes a few obscure matches. Acceptable for demo, and h2h Flamengo vs X wouldn't include PI club unless opponent matches.\n\nSimplest: if base ambiguous and no state given, keys = {base? no...} include all group keys sharing this base. Need mapping base → group keys. Precompute in SoccerData: self._base_groups: dict[base, set[group_keys]]. keys_for: if base in ambiguous and no state → return union of _base_groups[base] keys... but keys are group keys like \"flamengo rj\",\"flamengo pi\" — and _match_index is keyed by keys_for(name) of raw names, which for \"Flamengo-RJ\" = {\"flamengo rj\"} (base excluded since ambiguous). Good.\n\nAlso alias expansions: keys should also add alias members. _bare_keys handles that.\n\nRewrite keys_for:\n\n```python\ndef keys_for(self, name):\n    tokens, state = _base_and_state(name)\n    base = \" \".join(tokens)\n    keys = _bare_keys(name)  # includes base, base+state, aliases\n    if base in self.ambiguous_bases:\n        # exclude bare base; if no state given, expand to all clubs sharing base\n        keys = {k for k in keys if k != base}\n        if not state:\n            keys |= {g for g in self._base_groups.get(base, set())}\n            # also alias members of those groups\n            extra = set()\n            for g in keys: extra |= _ALIAS_LOOKUP.get(g, frozenset())\n            keys |= extra\n    if not keys:\n        keys = {group_key(name)}\n    return frozenset(keys)\n```\n\nHmm wait: if base ambiguous WITH state given: \"Atletico-MG\" → keys {\"atletico mg\"} (base excluded) ✓.\nWithout state: \"Atletico\" → keys = all atletico groups {\"atletico mg\",\"atletico pr\",\"atletico go\",\"atletico es\",\"atletico ac\"...} ✓ matches everything Atletico.\n\n\"Flamengo\" → {\"flamengo rj\",\"flamengo pi\"} plus aliases. h2h Flamengo vs Fluminense: Flamengo-PI never plays Fluminense presumably, fine.\n\nStandings unaffected (group_key).\n\nAmbiguity computation must use distinct non-empty states. Also for names without state (\"Flamengo\" in Libertadores), state \"\".\n\nAlso team_names/resolve use group_key — fine.\n\nOne more: _base_groups values should include group_key for every variant name including stateless ones (\"flamengo\" for \"Flamengo\"). If base ambiguous and query stateless, we add \"flamengo\" too (stateless Libertadores entries). In code above, _base_groups[base] includes group of every name with that base — including \"flamengo\" (stateless). Good.\n\nAlso fix average goals test: rounding sum != 100. Change test to use pytest.approx.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05d986f68001SrsW2de5CZ5GG9","sessionID":"ses_fa26fadadffe2UVfX81vYXdx41","messageID":"msg_05d9814a4001BbDFdggGvBCyoB"}}
